live-folder driver: late relays end by themselves, launchers told by command line, check.py bounded (#52 review)

- A relay spawned but not yet started at teardown has no start line, so stopRelays
  cannot find it; once stopLaunchers killed its launcher (the FIFO's only writer-to-be)
  it would wait in open() for good. The launcher now passes its pid ("$$", later the
  host's) to the relay, which watches it until the FIFO is open; once it is gone the
  relay opens the FIFO for writing without blocking and closes it, so its read open
  completes and reads the end of input (writerGone, then end). Bytes a host wrote just
  before exiting are still forwarded first. This also covers the browser ending a launch
  before its host started.
- stopLaunchers decides by the command line alone (the launcher script, not the host),
  never by live: off Linux liveness is kill(pid, 0), so on macOS a launcher waiting on
  its FIFO read as a live host and was never killed.
- check.py runs through runOracle, bounded by CHECK_MS (killed with SIGKILL): a hang
  now fails the run, which is then torn down, instead of never reaching teardown.

Tests (each fails on d8f4c28): a relay released only after teardown killed its launcher
is gone with its end logged; stopLaunchers on macOS's branches (platform darwin, no
/proc, ps) kills the waiting launcher; a check.py that never exits is killed at its bound.

